  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM EST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nCurrently-Tonight... KMLB radar imagery shows dry conditions\nacross east central Florida. GOES-19 satellite imagery and local\nobservations indicate mostly clear to partly cloudy skies.\nAnalysis charts show a \"cool front\" over the Treasure Coast which\nextends from low pressure (1008mb) situated near the northeastern\nUS coast. Temperatures are currently in the mid 70s to mid 80s\nwith dew points in the low 50s to low 70s. The dry weather is\nexpected to persist through the night across east central Florida\nwith seasonable to slightly above low temperatures in the upper\n50s to around 70F degrees. \n\nSunday-Monday... A rather persistent forecast is expected through\nthe end of the weekend and into the workweek. Substantial dry air\nis expected to filter across east central Florida with north-\nnortheast winds on Sunday shifting east-northeast on Monday at\n5-10mph. Meanwhile, the pattern is expected to remain mostly zonal\nat 300mb over the southern US which will help keep things quiet \nfor east central Florida. Afternoon highs in the low 80s to mid \n80s with lows in the upper 50s to near 70F degrees. \n\nTuesday-Friday... Moisture is expected to increase Tuesday and\nthrough late week with PWATs between 1.0\"-1.50\" Tuesday and\nbetween 1.30\"-1.70\" Wednesday before deterministic models begin \nto diverge in solutions with the next cold front Thursday into \nFriday. Isolated to widely scattered off and on onshore moving \nshowers are forecast on Tuesday. Mostly dry weather is forecast on\nWednesday. Deterministic models show two different scenarios on \nThursday into Friday with the GFS and CMC showing a deeper low \nthat will bring widespread rainfall to east central Florida with the\nECMWF indicating a weaker low and drier weather for east central \nFlorida. The LREF-ensemble clusters are in decent agreement and \nare closer to the GFS and CMC solution, therefore, at least a \nslight increase in rain chances is likely as we get closer to \nThursday into Friday. Seasonable to slightly above temperatures \nare forecast.",
